        	         [-evaluate_in_trace < property_name_tcl_list>]
        	         [-cex_limit <number_of_failures>]
        	       |-instance <instance_name>
        	         [-except <subinstance_name>+]
        	       | <expression>
        	      )
        	      [-from <property_name> [-trace_id <N>]
        	        [-cycle <N>]]
        	      [-on_determined <proc> ]
        	      [-with_helpers]
        	      [-with_proven]
        	      [-bg]
        	      [-force]
        	      [-asserts | -covers]
        	      [-quiet]
        	      [-soft_constraints <signal_name_tcl_list> [-extend_region]]
        	      [-soft_low_constraints <signal_name_tcl_list> [-extend_region]]
        	      [-engine_mode <engine_mode_tcl_list>]
        	      [-iter <N>]
        	      [-first_trace_attempt <N>]
        	      [-per_property_max_time_limit <time_limit>]
        	      [-per_property_time_limit <time_limit>]
        	      [-per_property_time_limit_factor <N>]
        	      [-time_limit <time_limit>]
        	      [-verbosity <N> |-silent]
        	      [-per_engine_max_jobs <N>]
        	      [-max_jobs <N>]
        	      
        	      [-no_traces]
        	      [-no_cover_traces]
        	      [-prefer_shortest]
        	      [-prefer_quiet]
        	      [-simplification_only]
        	      [-dry_run]
        	      [-dump_trace
        	       [-dump_trace_type (shm | vcd | fsdb)]
        	       [-dump_trace_dir <dir_name>]]
        	      [-with_ppd
        	       [<path_to_ppd_file>]]
        	      [-save_ppd
        	       [<path_to_ppd_file>]]
        	      [-orchestration (on | off)]
        	      [-sst [<N>]]
        	
        	When a proof is running in the background:
        	
        	prove ( -stop [<[index.]engine_mode>]
        	       |-kill <index.engine_mode>
        	       |-wait
        	       |-status [-silent]
        	       |-per_engine_max_jobs <N>
        	       |-add <engine_mode>
        	       |-restart <[index.]engine_mode>
        	       |-ignore_property <property_name_tcl_list>
        	      )
        	      [-thread <thread_id>]

Summary:	Proves properties, or controls a "prove" command running in the background.
Associated App(s):	Depending on the property to be proven.
Returns:	When proving properties without "-bg" these are the return values:
        	
        	Return Value       Definition
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	proven, covered    All properties have the same determined status.
        	cex, unreachable   The return is one of the values listed.
        	ar_covered, ar_cex
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	determined         All properties are fully determined, but they
        	                   have different statuses   ("proven", "cex",
        	                   "unreachable", "covered").
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	determined_or_skipped
        	                   Some properties were skipped (are ignored by the
        	                   selected proof setting) or left undetermined. See
        	                   "prove -from". All other properties are
        	                   fully determined.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	ar_determined      All properties are determined or "ar_covered" or
        	                   "ar_cex", and at least one property is
        	                   "ar_covered" or "ar_cex".
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	overconstrained    At least one task has inconsistent assumptions or
        	                   clock and reset.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	unprocessed        At least one property was left unprocessed.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	per_property_time_limit
        	time_limit         time_limit or per_property_time_limit has expired.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	max_trace_length   max_trace_length (iter) is reached.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	stopped_by_user    The prove command was stopped by the user.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	out_of_memory      At least one engine ran out of memory.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	internal_limit     Internal limit exceeded.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	engineJ_attempts   Engine J failed to satisfy all constraints, see
        	                   "set_engineJ_attempts", "set_engineJ_restarts".
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	no_properties      The given selection contained no properties.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	background         The prove command is now running in the background.
        	
        	                   NOTE: You can use the Application menu to put a
        	                   foreground proof into the background (Application -
        	                   Proof - Run Job in Background). When you do this,
        	                   the tool returns "background" to the console. The
        	                   Application menus of the following Apps do not have
        	                   this option: CDC, Coverage, FSV, SPV, Superlint
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	background thread_id
        	                   The "prove" command is running in the background
        	                   and is associated with the specified "thread_id".
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	aborted            The prove command stopped due to a ProofGrid error.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	failed             The prove command stopped due to an error.
        	-----------------  ------------------------------------------------
        	marked_proven      The property was created with "assert -mark_proven".
See also:	get_status,
set_engine_mode,
set_max_trace_length,
set_proof_simplification,
set_proofgrid_mode,
set_proofgrid_per_engine_max_jobs,
set_proofgrid_per_engine_max_local_jobs,
set_prove_orchestration,
set_prove_per_property_time_limit,
set_prove_time_limit,
set_proven_directive,
visualize

Detailed description:
Use this command to determine if assertions are "proven" or "cex" (or
"ar_cex") and cover properties are "unreachable" or "covered" (or
"ar_covered").

Important information about this command:
. "prove" does not support FSV. The tool issues an error in this case.
  Use "check_fsv -prove" instead.
. "prove" does not support SPV or X-Prop properties. The tool issues a
  warning in these cases and ignores the SPV or X-Prop properties respectively.
  Use "check_spv -prove" or "check_xprop -prove" instead.
. When a property result becomes "cex", "ar_cex", "covered", or "ar_covered",
  this command generates a trace "triggering" the property. See "visualize".
. The main switches control which properties to prove. Properties that are
  disabled are accepted but ignored. See "assert -disable", "cover -disable".
. A number of commands, most notably "set_engine_mode" and "set_proofgrid_mode"
  control _how_ property results are determined.
. By default, all engines leverage proven properties as assumptions for the
  proof of other properties proven together.
. You can monitor the proof progress by watching the Properties pane, Console
  tab, Proof Messages tab, and the ProofGrid Manager window.
. Also use this command to control a "prove" command running in the
  background (see "prove -bg").

NOTE: Determining property results can consume considerable time and
memory.

-all [-cex_limit number_of_failures]
    Prove all assertions and cover properties in all tasks.
    Use "-all" with "-cex_limit" to specify that you want the prove
    flow to stop the proof job after it reaches a certain threshold of
    failures on FPV properties. If the prove flow stops on cex_limit,
    it returns "determined_or_skipped".
    NOTE: "prove -all" issues an error message if used in a environment
    with Proof Structure nodes as it includes nodes that might not require
    proof. In such cases, do one of the following:
    . Use "prove -task" and specify the tasks/nodes instead
    . Use the utility from the Tcl library to prove necessary nodes
    (::jasper::psu::prove_all or ::jasper::psu::prove_all_serial)
    . Use "set_message -warning EPST052" to downgrade the message to a warning

-task [task_name_tcl_list] [-cex_limit number_of_failures]
    Prove all assertions and cover properties in the specified tasks.
    Use "-task" with "-cex_limit" to specify that you want the prove
    flow to stop the proof job after it reaches a certain threshold of
    failures on FPV properties. If the prove flow stops on cex_limit,
    it returns "determined_or_skipped".

    NOTE:
    . If you do not supply a task name, the tool proves all assertions and
      covers in the current task.
    . When specifying a list of task names, enclose the list in curly braces.
    . This switch supports wildcards (* and ?).

-property property_name_tcl_list [-regexp]
  [-evaluate_in_trace property_name_tcl_list]
  [-cex_limit number_of_failures]
    Prove the specified properties.
    Use "-property" with "-cex_limit" to specify that you want the prove
    flow to stop the proof job after it reaches a certain threshold of
    failures on FPV properties. If the prove flow stops on cex_limit,
    it returns "determined_or_skipped".

    NOTE:
    . To get maximal control over which proven properties are used as
      assumptions, use the default "set_proven_directive true", do not use
      "-with_proven" (see definition below), and do include the proven
      properties you want to use in the list of properties. In this case, the
      tool uses only the proven properties that are on your list.
    . To prove properties not in the current task prepend the property
      names with "<task>::".
    . All listed properties must belong to the same task.
    . This switch supports wildcards and regular expressions; for example:
      prove -property {expression} -regexp
    . Use "-evaluate_in_trace" to include a set of properties for the tool to
      evaluate when it finds a cex or a cover for one or more specified target
      properties. In general, the tool discovers the status of these evaluated
      properties by checking the traces found for the primary target
      properties. That is, the formal proof does not explicitly target them.

-instance instance_name [-except subinstance_name+]
    Prove all assertions and cover properties in the specified module
    instance and its associated subinstances, except the specified
    subinstances. The module subinstance names are relative to the
    module instance name; that is, you will not specify a full path.

    NOTE: Embedded properties, which are declared in the RTL, belong to a
    specific instance, and the tool extracts them to the task <embedded> during
    elaboration. For this reason, "prove -instance" only applies to
    properties that belong to the <embedded> task, which must be the
    current task ("task -set <embedded>").

expression
    If an assertion with the specified expression already exists, prove that;
    otherwise, use the same behavior as "prove -property [assert expression]".
    See "assert".

-from property_name [-trace_id N] [-cycle N]
    Use an existing trace as the initial value for another formal proof.

    . Use "-trace_id" to specify a trace other than the default. The default
      property trace is the last shortest trace. See "assert/cover
      -set_store_trace" for the commands to configure the  number of traces
      generated and stored by the tool.

    . Use "-cycle" to specify the state of the trace at a specific cycle as the
      reset value. If you do not specify a cycle, or if you specify 0, the tool
      uses the cycle in which the property was triggered. This is often, but
      not always, the last cycle of the trace. See "set_trace_extension".

      If you specify $, or if you specify -1, the tool uses the last cycle of
      the existing trace. A negative N selects the cycle relative to the end
      of the trace.

    Use this feature when a formal proof finds an interesting scenario (for
    example, a counterexample or complex cover) and you want to perform another
    search from that point. By doing so, you might find scenarios that are
    deeper than what you could reach with other formal proofs.

    NOTE:
    . This is a bug-hunting feature. The tool is not able to prove assertions
      or determine that covers are unreachable when you use "prove -from".
    . Found traces will start from reset and include the trace to the
      specified property as a prefix.
    . If undetermined properties are triggered in the existing trace, the trace
      will be used as the result for those properties. (The first time a
      property is triggered along a trace is the most significant event.)

-on_determined proc
    Override the current "on_determined" for this "prove"
    command. See "set_prove_on_determined".

-with_helpers
    Prove with helper assertions.

    You can prioritize helper assertions over regular assertions by submitting
    them first to the engines with "assert -helper...".

-with_proven
    Use all proven properties in the same task as assumptions.

    This switch offers a convenient way to specify that you want the tool to
    use all proven properties regardless of the properties you list and the
    current "proven_directive" setting.

-bg
    Run the "prove" command in the background and continue to accept
    and run new commands.

-force
    Clear existing proof results for the selected properties before
    beginning. See "clear -result".

-asserts
    Prove only assertions, not covers.

-covers
    Prove only covers, not assertions.

-quiet
    Use all undriven signals as extra soft low constraints. This is a
    complement to "assume -soft", "prove -soft_constraints", and "prove
    -soft_low_constraints" that offers more convenience but less control.

-soft_constraints signal_name_tcl_list [-extend_region]
    Use the selected signals (those matching any of the list of
    wildcards) as extra soft constraints.

    Important things to know:
    . Use this switch for signals you prefer to be high in traces, see "assume
      -soft".
    . Use "-extend_region" to prevent the tool from ignoring out-of-region
      signals and issuing a warning.
    . This switch supports wildcards (* and ?) in signal names.
    . This switch supports Tcl lists as arguments.

-soft_low_constraints signal_name_tcl_list [-extend_region]
    Use the selected signals (those matching any of the list of
    wildcards) as extra soft low constraints.

    Important things to know:
    . Use this switch for signals you prefer to be low in traces, see "assume
      -soft".
    . Use "-extend_region" to prevent the tool from ignoring out-of-region
      signals and issuing a warning.
    . This switch supports wildcards (* and ?) in signal names.
    . This switch supports Tcl lists as arguments.

-engine_mode engine_mode_tcl_list
    Override the current "engine_mode" for this "prove"
    command. See "set_engine_mode".

-iter N
    Override the current "max_trace_length" for this "prove"
    command. See "set_max_trace_length".

    NOTE: You can also use this command to specify a "bounded proof", that is,
    a proof that terminates after reaching the specified iteration.

-first_trace_attempt
    Override the current "first_trace_attempt" for this "prove"
    command. See "set_first_trace_attempt".

-per_property_max_time_limit time_limit
    Override the current "prove_per_property_max_time_limit" for this
    "prove" command. See "set_prove_per_property_max_time_limit".

-per_property_time_limit time_limit
    Override the current "prove_per_property_time_limit" for this
    "prove" command. See "set_prove_per_property_time_limit".

-per_property_time_limit_factor N
    Override the current "prove_per_property_time_limit_factor" for this
    "prove" command. See "set_prove_per_property_time_limit_factor".

-time_limit time_limit
    Override the current "prove_time_limit" for this "prove"
    command. See "set_prove_time_limit".

-verbosity N
    Override the current "prove_verbosity" for this "prove"
    command. See "set_prove_verbosity".

-silent
    Alias for -verbosity 0.

-per_engine_max_jobs N
    Run the specified number of engine_jobs per engine.

    NOTE: This switch overrides proofgrid_per_engine_max_jobs and
    proofgrid_per_engine_max_local_jobs. See
    "set_proofgrid_per_engine_max_jobs" and
    "set_proofgrid_per_engine_max_local_jobs".

-max_jobs N
    Run the specified number of engine_jobs.

    NOTE: This switch overrides "proofgrid_max_jobs" and
    "proofgrid_max_local_jobs". See "set_proofgrid_max_jobs" and
    "set_proofgrid_max_local_jobs".

-no_traces
-no_cover_traces
    Do not generate any traces or cover traces specifically.

    Use this switch to avoid the overhead of generating and storing traces.
    The properties will show cex/covered statuses as usual, but there will be
    no traces to inspect. You must re-do the proof to get traces. Use this
    switch when the trace data is not important.

    NOTE: If the tool finds a trace for an assertion, and the trace also
    contains a witness for a cover property, you will be able to open the trace
    for the cover property despite specifying "-no_cover_traces".

-prefer_shortest
    Override the current "prove_prefer_shortest" for this "prove"
    command. See "set_prove_prefer_shortest".

-prefer_quiet
    Override the current "prove_prefer_quiet" for this "prove"
    command. See "set_prove_prefer_quiet".

-simplification_only
    Run only preprocessing and simplification without invoking the regular
    formal engines.

    NOTE:
    . You can use this switch heuristically, in parallel with the regular
      engines, to determine properties that are trivial for simplification.
    . "prove -simplification_only" and "prove -engine_mode" are mutually
      exclusive (within the same command).
    . "prove -simplification_only" overrides the current "proof_simplification"
      setting. See "set_proof_simplification".
    . "prove -simplification_only" restores results from the proof cache if
      "prove_cache" is on. You can prevent this behavior by running
      "set_prove_cache off". (The default setting is "off".)

-dry_run
    Compute an upper bound on the number of engine jobs that could be
    launched by the proof if it was run.

    The return value is a list of name/value pairs of the bound of the total
    number of jobs as well as a bound for each engine in the proof. The return
    is suitable for use with Tcl "dict".

    NOTE: This feature does not work with proof orchestration. See
    "set_prove_orchestration".

-dump_trace
    [-dump_trace_type (shm | vcd | fsdb)]
    [-dump_trace_dir dir_name]
    Dump traces to the disk as soon as the engines find them. The tool uses
    the SHM format as the default and writes files in the project directory.

    . Use "-dump_trace_type" with "shm", "vcd", or "fsdb" to
      specify the file dump type. VCD files are dumped as compressed files to
      save disk space.

      IMPORTANT:
      . Jasper Apps writes 5.7 FSDB traces and reads version 5.9 FSDB
        traces. FSDB 5.9 was introduced by Verdi version R-2020.12.
      . FSDBs you generate with the latest Jasper are backwards compatible
        with all Jasper versions from 2018.12 FCS and newer. In addition,
        they are backwards compatible with older versions of Verdi released
        during that same period.
      . Jasper Apps supports backwards compatibility, that is, the tool can
        read FSDBs generated from older versions of Jasper Apps and
        Synopsys Verdi.
      

    . Use "-dump_trace_dir" to specify the dump directory where the files will
      be written.

    NOTE: If you have also used the set_trace_optimization command, the trace
    file reflects those settings, for example:
    . If trace optimization is ar, then only the analysis region is saved into
      the file. This option is the default.
    . If you use set_trace_optimization standard, then the entire design is
      dumped into the file. However, you should be aware that this setting can
      increase the time it takes to generate a trace.

-with_ppd
    [path_to_ppd_file]
    Read proof profiling data (PPD) previously saved with "-save_ppd".
    The tool uses this information to make future runs faster and consume fewer
    resources.

    NOTE:
    . If you use "-with_ppd" without the path_to_ppd_file argument, the tool
      uses the "sessionPPD.ppd" file in the session directory.
    . If you use "-with_ppd", and the ppd_file_name contains information from
      multiple runs, the tool attempts to find the most relevant information
      for the running proof, considering information from all previous runs.
    . If you use "-with_ppd" and Proof Orchestration is disabled, the tool
      enables it and prints a warning. However, if you explicitly disable Proof
      Orchestration, the tool issues an error.

-save_ppd
    [path_to_ppd_file]
    Save the PPD for use with future runs to potentially make them run faster
    and consume fewer resources.

    If you use "-save_ppd" without the path_to_ppd_file argument, the tool
    writes the information to a default PPD file named "sessionPPD.ppd" in the
    session directory.

    NOTE:
    . If you use "-save_ppd", and the ppd_file_name is not empty, the tool
      appends new information to the existing information.
    . If you use "-save_ppd" and Proof Orchestration is disabled, the tool
      enables it and prints a warning. However, if you explicitly disable Proof
      Orchestration, the tool issues an error.

-orchestration (on | off)
    Override the current "prove_orchestration" setting for this "prove"
    command. See "set_prove_orchestration".

-sst [N]
    Assuming that all enabled helper- and sst-properties in the task pass the
    first few cycles, run the proof on the helpers without reset and without
    bounded assumptions (see "assume -bound"). Properties are allowed to fail at
    the cycle specified by "N", which represents the minimum trace length. If
    "N" is not provided, properties are allowed to fail starting at the cycle
    specified by the "set_sst_default_trace_length" command.

    For properties with a trace extension, properties are allowed to fail
    earlier in the SST trace (see "assert -set_trace_extension" and
    "set_trace_extension"). This means that such properties are required to
    pass for fewer cycles. In particular, if the extension exceeds the SST trace
    length, the property is allowed to fail anywhere and does not effectively
    constrain the trace.

    In all cases, the traces generated might be longer than the minimum
    specified.

    If a trace is found for a property, it is delivered as an SST trace, and the
    property remains undetermined. By inspecting the SST trace and basing
    another helper assertion on it, you might learn the different failure modes
    on rerunning the proof. This could result in no trace possible.
    An SST trace generated by a "prove" command does not support Visualize
    constraints or Visualize replot.

    Resets are kept as soft constraints, so with "-prefer_quiet", SST traces
    starting close to reset are generated.

    If a proof is found, it is reported as an "sst_max_length" bound, see
    "get_property_info -list sst_max_length". If all properties have a
    sufficient "min_length" bound, a full proven result is established. If only
    a single property is being assumed, then a normal single property
    "max_length" bound is reported. If an "sst_max_length" is achieved for all
    helper properties then a full proof can be achieved by first running a
    normal non-sst proof establishing "min_length" bounds surpassing the
    "sst_max_length", and then finally run another "prove -sst" drawing the
    final conclusion. The final proof is trivial.

    NOTE:
    . Properties can be made helper properties using "assert -set_helper".
    . Selected properties that are not already helpers become sst-properties.
    . The cost and effectiveness of a SST proof depend on
      "set_sst_default_trace_length" and the full set of enabled helper
      properties in the task. You can explicitly include helper properties
      using one of the following:
        . "-with_helpers" switch
        . "-property <property name>"
        . "-with_proven" switch
        . "-task" or "-all"
    . Helper and sst properties are always included in the proof, but will play
      an active or passive role depending on whether they are explicitly included
      in the property list or included using "with_helpers". The roles:
        . Active: The property is assumed in the first few cycles, but also
          proven.
        . Passive: The property is assumed in the first few cycles, but then
          evaluated in trace.

     See also "get_property_info", "get_property_list", "visualize -sst",
     "set_sst_default_trace_length", "prove -with_helpers",
     "prove -with_proven", and "prove -evaluate_in_trace".

-stop [engine_mode | index.engine_mode] [-thread thread_id]
    Stop the entire "prove" command running in the background or a single
    engine_mode or engine_job (index.engine_mode).

    Use "-thread" to stop a proof thread or an engine_mode or engine_job that
    is associated with a specified thread.

    NOTE:
    . "-stop" is not blocking and might return before the proof has actually
      stopped. If you want blocking behavior, use "prove -stop; prove -wait",
      see below.
    . "prove -stop" stops all running proof threads.
    . Stopping all engines in a proof is equivalent to stopping the proof.

-kill index.engine_mode [-thread thread_id]
    Kill the proofgrid_shell process for the specified engine job.

    Use "-thread" to kill a proof thread or an engine_mode or engine_job that
    is associated with a specified thread.

    NOTE: Depending on the nature of the proofgrid_shell you are using (see
    "set_proofgrid_shell"), killing a job can have drawbacks. Therefore, you
    should only use this command as a last resort when
    "-stop index.engine_mode" does not appear to be working.

-wait [-thread thread_id]
    Wait for the currently running "prove" command to complete before running
    additional commands.

    Use "-thread" to tell the tool to wait for the "prove" command running
    under the specified thread to complete before running additional commands.

    NOTE:
    . "-wait" without "-thread" waits for all running background proofs to
      complete.
    . "-wait -thread" waits for the specified thread to complete.

    This option is useful when "prove" is running in the background (-bg). In
    effect, it moves "prove" back to the foreground, and the tool accepts no
    additional commands until the proof completes.

    "prove -wait" returns the value the "prove" command would have
    returned if it had run without "-bg" (see above). It returns
    this value even if the previously started proof has already
    stopped or never ran in the background.

-status [-thread thread_id] [-silent]
    List the running engine jobs and their statuses.

    . Use "-thread" to list the status of a specified thread's running engine
      jobs.
    . Use "-silent" to turn off the output to the screen and return information
      with a Tcl return value, which is helpful in Tcl scripts.

    The following list shows prove statuses.
    . "NeverStarted": Nothing has run in this session.
    . "Pending": There is currently a prove about to start.
    . "Simplify": A prove is currently running proof simplification.
    . "Running engineJob*": A prove is running with the listed number of
      engineJobs currently running.
    . "Running threadStatus*": A prove is running with the listed threads, where
      threadStatus is the status of a thread in the list.
    . "Finishing": Job is about to finish. Interactive commands sent to this
      job are ignored.
    . "Completed": No job is currently running. The last prove has completed.
    . "licenseStarved": A job stopped because, for example, it lost a license.

    NOTE: "prove -status" lists the status for all running engine jobs for all
    proof threads. The tool prefixes statuses with the associated thread ID.

-per_engine_max_jobs N [-thread thread_id]
    Dynamically change the per_engine_max_jobs setting for the running
    "prove" command.

    Use "-thread" to change the setting for the "prove" that is associated with
    a specified thread.

-add engine_mode [-thread thread_id]
    Add the specified engine_mode to the running "prove" command.

    Use "-thread" to add the specified engine_mode to the "prove" that is
    associated with a specified thread.

-restart (engine_mode | index.engine_mode) [-thread thread_id]
    Stop and restart the specified engine or engine job.

    Use "-thread" to stop and restart the proof thread or an engine_mode or
    engine_job that is associated with a specified thread.

-ignore_property property_name_tcl_list [-thread thread_id]
    Instruct engines in the running "prove" command to stop processing the
    specified properties.

    NOTE:
    . Use "-thread" to ignore properties only in the specified thread;
      otherwise, "prove -ignore_property" affects all running proof threads.
    . This switch supports wildcards and regular expressions, for example:
      prove -ignore_property {precondition1$} -regexp
    . Wildcard matching on this command only matches properties in the current
      task. To match multiple tasks at once, use the scope separator "::", for
      example:
      prove -ignore_property {*my_task*::*my_prop*}

Example:
% prove -all
% prove -task taskA -engine_mode D
% prove -task { taskA taskB taskC }
% prove  { mon.master_error == 1'b0 }
% prove -instance instA -except subinstB
% prove -task taskA -save_ppd -with_ppd

# Specify multiple engines for parallel processing for faster run time
% prove -task taskA -engine_mode { d g h }

# Instruct all running single-property engines to stop processing propertyA
% prove -ignore_property { propertyA }

